# When a price rises, why do you buy less — for two reasons?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What are the substitution and the income effects? Can they pull in opposite directions?"
next: {"title":"Can a price rise make you buy more?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/77-can-a-price-rise-make-you-buy-more.cast"}

## Coffee or rent?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Ground coffee
      - Rent
    values:
      - 124
      - 33
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      prefix: +
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    icons:
      - coffee
      - house
    colors:
      - orange
      - red
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 140
    y_label: Price rise since Jan 2020 (%)
    x_label: US, Jan 2020 → Sep 2026
    label_size: 28
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 160
      w: 500
      h: 480
sources:
    - id: blscoffee
      title: 'Average Price: Coffee, 100%, Ground Roast, All Sizes, per pound, U.S. City Average (APU0000717311)'
      authors: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, via FRED
      year: 2026
      finding: '$4.17 per pound in January 2020, $9.33 in September 2026: +124%.'
      url: https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/APU0000717311
    - id: blsrent
      title: 'Consumer Price Index: Rent of Primary Residence in U.S. City Average (CUSR0000SEHA)'
      authors: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, via FRED
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Index 337.6 in January 2020, 450.4 in September 2026: +33%.'
      url: https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/CUSR0000SEHA
    - id: blsce
      title: Consumer Expenditures in 2024
      authors: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics
      year: 2025
      finding: Average annual spending per household $78,535; nonalcoholic beverages (coffee, tea, soft drinks, juice) $714, about 0.9%; housing 33.4%.
      url: https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cesan_12192025.htm

    scratch calc cites ["blsce"] work ["poorer ≈ rise × share","coffee: 124% × <1% → <1%","rent: 33% × ⅓ ≈ 11%"] x 805 y 440 font_size 27 hidden true
    text why1 "1. dearer than the rest" #b5482e x 810 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    text why2 "2. less money overall" #2f6b8f x 810 y 400 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Coffee or rent?"

When a price goes up, why do you buy less? Two American prices since January 2020 make a good test.
    draw axes

Ground coffee: from  (@draw bar_1@)about four dollars a pound to over nine. Up a hundred and twenty-four percent.

    pause 0.3

    ask "Drag the rent bar to how much you think rents rose." on bar_2 store g default "70"
        intro: Now rent, on the same scale. How much do you think American rents rose over the same years?
        right: Close: you said {g}%, and rents rose {g.true}%.
        wrong: You said {g}%; rents rose {g.true}%, a gap of {g.off} points.

Rent rose by a third. A much smaller jump than coffee.
    draw bar_2

B: Then the answer is easy. You buy less because you can't afford as much.
    point at.ref bar_2 at.anchor top gesture tap

    ask "Which rise made a typical household poorer? Tap its bar." choose ["bar_1","bar_2"] answer bar_2
        intro: That's what most people say, and it's half the answer. Try this: which of the two rises made households poorer?
        right: Rent.
        wrong: Think about how much of a household's budget each one takes: here, rent.

Rent, by far. How much poorer a pr (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@)ice rise makes you is roughly the rise times the share of your budget it takes.

Coffee and ev (@draw calc_line_2@)ery other drink together are under one percent of what American households spen (@draw calc_line_3@)d. Rent, for a renter, might be a third.

B: So the coffee drinker who switches to tea isn't doing it because she's poorer.
    highlight calc_line_2

Hardly at all. She switches because coffee now c (@draw why1@)osts more compared with tea and everything else.
    erase calc

And the renter who takes a smaller fla (@draw why2@)t is also poorer: there's simply less money to go round.

Every price rise does both at once. To see how much of each, we need to pull them apart.
    highlight why1 why2

## Splitting the move
use: indifference_budget
with:
    income: 2400
    price_x: 30
    price_x_before: 20
    price_y: 1
    preference: balanced
    curves: 1
    x_good: Space (m²)
    y_good: Other things ($)
    x_max: 140
    y_max: 2600
    axis_numbers: true
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 140
      w: 700
      h: 520

    scratch readout work ["swap ≈ 11 m²","poorer ≈ 9 m²"] x 830 y 480 font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch mth work ["ΔQ = substitution + income","income ≈ −Q · ΔP · ΔQ/ΔI","big share → big effect"] x 800 y 480 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Splitting the move"

    hide guide_y

Take a renter, with illustrative numbers: two thousand four hundred dollars a month, and she likes to spend half of it on floor space.
    draw axes

At twenty do (@draw budget_old label_budget_old ic_old label_ic_old point_a label_point_a guide_a@)llars a square metre, the best she can reach is point A: sixty square metres, on this indifference curve.

Now rent rises to thirty dollars a square m (@draw budget_line label_budget@)etre. Her budget line swings in: the same money buys less space.

B: And her new b (@draw ic_1 label_ic_1 optimum optimum_label guide_x@)est is B, on a lower curve: forty square metres. Twenty fewer. Which part is the swap, and which is being poorer?

Here's the trick. In our heads, hand her just enough money to be as happy as before, a (@draw budget_comp label_budget_comp@)t the new prices: a line with the new slope, slid out until it touches her old curve.

B: It touches at (@draw point_c label_point_c guide_c@) C: about forty-nine square metres. Same happiness as A, yet less space.

    camera on ["point_a","optimum","point_c","substitution_effect","income_effect","label_substitution","label_income"] duration 1.2

A to C is the su (@draw substitution_effect label_substitution@)bstitution effect. Space now costs more in other things, so she swaps some away; on the same curve, a steeper line can only touch further left.

B: And C to B is t (@draw income_effect label_income@)he income effect: take the imaginary money back, and being poorer trims her space again.

    camera reset true duration 1

About eleven s (@draw readout_box readout_line_1@)quare metres from the swap, nine fro (@draw readout_line_2@)m being poorer. Space is half her budget, so being poorer counts almost as much as the swap.

Pause and drag the budget line's end on the space axis: the dearer the rent, the further B slides back.
    explore

    erase readout

    draw mathq

    ask "The arithmetic of the split: show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

The whole move is the two effects added together.
    draw mth_box mth_line_1

The income effect is roughly how much you buy, times the price rise, times how strongly your buying responds to income.
    draw mth_line_2

So it is big for things that take a big share of your budget, and tiny for coffee.
    draw mth_line_3

    pause 0.5

    erase mth

@after

    erase mathq

    pause 0.3

## A tug of war
    text less "← less" #8f887c x 440 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    text more "more →" #8f887c x 920 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    text qlab "Quantity bought" #3d3833 x 690 y 640 font_size 34 hidden true
    path start1 dashed #8f887c points [[690,505],[690,575]] hidden true
    icon house size 90 x 90 y 540 icon_look picture of house hidden true
    text lab1 "Floor space" #3d3833 x 225 y 540 font_size 34 hidden true
    arrow sub1 #b5482e from.x 690 from.y 552 to.x 490 to.y 552 style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    arrow inc1 #2f6b8f from.x 490 from.y 526 to.x 328 to.y 526 style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    text s1t "swap" #b5482e x 590 y 580 font_size 30 hidden true
    text i1t "poorer" #2f6b8f x 409 y 496 font_size 30 hidden true
    path start2 dashed #8f887c points [[690,335],[690,405]] hidden true
    icon bread size 90 x 90 y 370 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    text lab2 "Cheapest loaf" #3d3833 x 225 y 370 font_size 34 hidden true
    arrow sub2 #b5482e from.x 690 from.y 382 to.x 490 to.y 382 style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    arrow inc2 #2f6b8f from.x 490 from.y 356 to.x 615 to.y 356 style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    text s2t "swap" #b5482e x 590 y 410 font_size 30 hidden true
    text i2t "poorer" #2f6b8f x 553 y 326 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow net2 #3d3833 from.x 690 from.y 290 to.x 615 to.y 290 style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    text n2t "net: a little less" #3d3833 x 480 y 290 font_size 30 hidden true
    text inf "inferior good" #8a5fa8 x 225 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    path start3 dashed #8f887c points [[690,170],[690,240]] hidden true
    text lab3 "Bigger pull?" #8f887c x 225 y 205 font_size 34 hidden true
    arrow sub3 #b5482e from.x 690 from.y 217 to.x 540 to.y 217 style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    arrow inc3 #2f6b8f from.x 540 from.y 191 to.x 840 to.y 191 style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    text q3 "?" #8a5fa8 x 940 y 205 font_size 60 hidden true
    text norm "normal good" #8f887c x 225 y 500 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "A tug of war"

    draw less qlab more

For spa (@draw house lab1 start1 sub1 s1t inc1 i1t@)ce, both arrows pointed the same way: less. Must they always?

B: The substitution arrow must. Make a good dearer and hold happiness fixed, and you always swap some of it away.
    highlight sub1

And the income arrow? With more money, she'd rent more space.
    highlight inc1

B: That makes space a normal g (@draw norm@)ood. But some things you buy precisely because money is tight.

Say, in Harbourtown (@draw bread lab2 start2@), the corner shop's cheapest loaf. A family on a tight budget lives on it.

B: Give that family more money and they switch to proper bakery bread. A good you buy le (@draw inf@)ss of as you get richer is called inferior. No snobbery: it only names the direction.

So the shop rai (@draw sub2 s2t@)ses the price of its cheapest loaf.

    quiz "Through the income effect alone, does the family buy more or less of the cheap loaf?"
        right: More.
        wrong: Poorer, they can afford less bakery bread, so they lean on the cheap loaf: here, more.
        + More
        * Less
        * No change

B: More. Poor (@draw inc2 i2t@)er, the family can afford even less bakery bread, so they lean harder on the cheap loaf.

So here the two arrows pull in opposite directions.
    highlight sub2 inc2

B: And the net is whichever is longer. For a food that i (@draw net2 n2t@)s only part of the budget, the swap usually wins: they still buy a little less.

But what if the i (@draw start3 lab3 sub3 inc3@)ncome arrow were longer than the swap?

B: Then a higher price would make you (@draw q3@) buy more. It sounds impossible; whether it ever happens is a hunt of its own.

    fade start3 lab3 sub3 inc3 to 0.4

## Can we see C?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Slutsky would have nodded.
    good: Both arrows found, mostly.
    poor: 'The swap always points away from the dearer good: start there.'
    none: Even the rats got this one.
use: indifference_budget
with:
    income: 2400
    price_x: 30
    price_x_before: 20
    price_y: 1
    preference: balanced
    curves: 1
    x_good: Space (m²)
    y_good: Other things ($)
    x_max: 140
    y_max: 2700
    axis_numbers: true
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 140
      w: 650
      h: 520
sources:
    - id: kbrg1981
      title: Demand Curves for Animal Consumers
      authors: Kagel, J. H., Battalio, R. C., Rachlin, H. and Green, L.
      year: 1981
      finding: Both income-compensated and ordinary demand curves of laboratory animals slope downward.
    - id: kagel1975
      title: Experimental Studies of Consumer Demand Behavior Using Laboratory Animals
      authors: Kagel, J. H., Battalio, R. C., Rachlin, H., Green, L., Basmann, R. L. and Klemm, W. R.
      year: 1975
      finding: Laboratory animals shifted consumption toward the cheaper good when the budget set changed.
    - id: slutsky1915
      title: Sulla teoria del bilancio del consumatore
      authors: Slutsky, E.
      year: 1915
      finding: Splits a price effect into an effect at compensated income and an income effect; compensation keeps the old bundle affordable.
    - id: hicks1939
      title: Value and Capital
      authors: Hicks, J. R.
      year: 1939
      finding: Compensation that keeps the consumer on the same indifference curve.

    text hicks "Hicks: same happiness" #d0865f cites ["hicks1939"] x 820 y 330 font_size 26 hidden true
    text slut "Slutsky: can still afford A" #8a5fa8 cites ["slutsky1915"] x 820 y 285 font_size 26 hidden true
    arrow sline dashed thick #8a5fa8 from.data [0,3000] to.data [100,0] hidden true
    icon rat size 90 x 820 y 560 icon_look picture of rat hidden true
    text ratt1 "lab animals:" #3d3833 cites ["kbrg1981"] x 820 y 480 font_size 26 hidden true
    text ratt2 "compensated rise →" #3d3833 x 820 y 440 font_size 26 hidden true
    text ratt3 "still bought less" #3d3833 x 820 y 400 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c1 "substitution effect:" #3d3833 x 820 y 340 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c2 "consensus" #2f7d4f x 820 y 295 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c3 "income effect:" #3d3833 x 820 y 240 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c4 "depends on the good" #b5482e x 820 y 200 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Can we see C?"

    hide guide_y

    draw axes budget_old label_budget_old ic_old label_ic_old point_a label_point_a guide_a budget_line label_budget ic_1 label_ic_1 optimum optimum_label guide_x budget_comp label_budget_comp point_c label_point_c guide_c substitution_effect label_substitution income_effect label_income parallel true

    camera on ["point_a","optimum","point_c","substitution_effect","income_effect","label_substitution","label_income"] duration 1.2

B: One thing bothers me. Nobody has ever seen point C. It's an imaginary bundle at an imaginary income.
    highlight point_c effect circle

True. C is a thought experiment, and it hides a choice: what does 'just as well off' mean?
    highlight budget_comp

    camera reset true duration 1

One answer, John Hi (@draw hicks@)cks's: on the same indifference curve, equally happy. That's the line we drew.

B: Eugen Slutsky, back in  (@draw sline slut@)1915, said: enough money to still afford the old bundle A. Fewer assumptions about happiness.

For a sma (@@)ll price change the two lin (@highlight budget_comp sline@)es almost coincide; for a big one the split shifts a little. The two effects are a way of reading the move, not two things you can photograph.

    erase hicks slut

B: Then can we test it at all? From the 1970s, John Kagel, Raymond B (@draw rat ratt1@)attalio and colleagues let lab animals buy goods with lever presses, on a fixed budget of presses.

Raise one p (@draw ratt2 ratt3@)rice and top up the budget to compensate, and the animals still bought less of the dearer good. A downward substi (@erase rat ratt1 ratt2 ratt3@)tution effect is (@draw c1 c2@) about as close to consensus as economics gets; the income  (@draw c3 c4@)effect depends on the good.

Three new cases to try.
    fade sline to 0.3

    quiz "Harbourtown's council taxes ferry fuel and hands every household all the money back as a cheque. Will people use less fuel?"
        right: Yes: the cheque undoes being poorer, but fuel still costs more compared with everything else, so the substitution effect remains.
        wrong: The cheque undoes being poorer, but not the new price of fuel compared with everything else: here, yes, fuel still costs more than other things.
        * No: the cheque cancels the tax
        + Yes: fuel still costs more than other things
        * Only poor households will

    quiz "For a typical household, which has the bigger income effect?" voice b
        right: Rent rising ten percent: it takes a far bigger share of the budget than salt, so it makes you much poorer.
        wrong: Multiply the rise by the share of the budget it takes: here, rent rising 10%.
        * Salt doubles in price
        + Rent rises 10%
        * About the same

    quiz "Ana takes the bus to work because it is cheap; with more money she would drive. Bus fares rise. Which is true?"
        right: Substitution cuts it, the income effect raises it: for Ana the bus is an inferior good, so being poorer pushes her toward more bus rides.
        wrong: The substitution arrow never points toward the dearer good, and the bus is what she drops when richer: here, substitution cuts it, the income effect raises it.
        * Both effects cut her bus riding
        + Substitution cuts it, the income effect raises it
        * Substitution raises it, the income effect cuts it

    erase c1 c2 c3 c4

    camera on ["point_a","optimum","point_c","substitution_effect","income_effect","label_substitution","label_income"] duration 1.2

So why do you buy less when a price rises? Because the good now costs more than everything else, which always pushes you away, and because you are poorer, which usually does too.
    highlight substitution_effect income_effect

    camera reset true duration 1

For coffee it is nearly all the first; for rent, a good share of the second.
    highlight label_substitution

## Where next
