      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1emqz37'
    '|b||Then can we test it at all? From the 1970s, John Kagel, Raymond Battalio and colleagues let lab animals buy goods with lever presses, on a fixed budget of presses.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1afkuwt'
    '|a||Raise one price and top up the budget to compensate, and the animals still bought less of the dearer good. A downward substitution effect is about as close to consensus as economics gets; the income effect depends on the good.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1gl7p4r'
    '|a||Three new cases to try.':
